extends Reference

# Short, task-oriented pages. Diagrams are schematic, not screenshots of a run.
static func pages() -> Array:
	return [
		{"title": "Start here", "section": "Selection", "picture": "window", "caption": "Move the title bar. Resize with the bottom-right handle.",
		"steps": "1. Open a level in the normal editor, then open CREATE → Editor+.\n2. Select objects in the game editor. Shift-click adds to a selection; dragging a box selects several objects.\n3. Choose a section from the dropdown below Undo/Redo. Changes act on the current selection.",
		"example": "Select two blocks, open Selection, set Move step to 0.25, then press →. Both blocks move right together.",
		"tip": "Drag the Editor+ title to move the window. The ↘ corner changes its width/height; the Scale row changes text and control size. Fit keeps it on screen. Reset or Ctrl+0 restores a usable layout."},
		{"title": "Position, rotation & pivot", "section": "Selection", "picture": "move", "caption": "A shared nudge preserves the spacing between selected objects.",
		"steps": "1. Select one or more objects. Mixed means their current values differ.\n2. For a relative move, set Move step and use ← ↑ ↓ →.\n3. For an exact value, enter Position X/Y, Rotation ° or Pivot X/Y and press that row's Apply (or Enter).\n4. Shape ±90° rotates the object's native shape orientation.",
		"example": "To move two blocks one grid unit to the right without changing their spacing, use step 1 and →. Setting Position X to 10 instead gives both blocks X = 10.",
		"tip": "Rotation is in degrees. Pivot is normalized: 0.5, 0.5 is the center; 0, 0 is top-left. It sets the center of rotation. Use the native Copy/Paste buttons to duplicate objects with their geometry and tweens. Undo/Ctrl+Z reverses edits or paste; Redo/Ctrl+Y reapplies them."},
		{"title": "Size & mirror", "section": "Selection", "picture": "size", "caption": "Half-size geometry is genuinely smaller, not a visual-only scale.",
		"steps": "1. Select a supported object and enter Width or Height in grid units. Apply each row separately.\n2. Static blocks, ice blocks and ramps support 0.25–1000 units. Their small edge handles snap to quarter units.\n3. Animated versions, backgrounds/ramps, jump zones, gravity fields and disappearing blocks resize from 1 unit upward.\n4. Flip H mirrors left/right; Flip V mirrors up/down around the selection's combined bounds.",
		"example": "For a half-height ledge, select a static block, set Width to 4 and Height to 0.5. For a mirrored obstacle arrangement, select the supported objects together and press Flip H.",
		"tip": "Flips also reflect supported movement/rotation tween paths; artwork itself is not texture-flipped. Floors/spikes support flipping, but not these size controls. Unsupported mixed selections change nothing. Sub-unit objects must stay static and need Goobplayability to keep exact geometry."},
		{"title": "Shared properties", "section": "Properties", "picture": "properties", "caption": "One shared property edit can update several compatible objects.",
		"steps": "1. Select objects and open Properties.\n2. Choose a property from the dropdown. Only compatible properties shared by every selected object appear.\n3. Enter a value or choose an option, then press Apply to selection.\n4. Use Undo to restore each object's previous value, including a previously mixed selection.",
		"example": "Select two gravity fields, choose strength, enter 3 and apply. Both use strength 3. Or select a sign and edit its message property.",
		"tip": "The game defines the available properties and their allowed ranges. A missing property cannot be invented here. Native inspector property edits also use the same undo history."},
		{"title": "Animate with tweens", "section": "Tweens", "picture": "tween", "caption": "Two keys move out and back; Repeat runs that path again.",
		"steps": "1. Select a supported object at least 1 unit in size. Choose Movement or Rotation.\n2. Press + Key. Choose its Key number, then Apply Duration and Value. Movement values are X, Y offsets; rotation values are degrees.\n3. + Delay adds a timed pause. ↑/↓ reorder the selected key; Delete removes it.\n4. Set Repeat On and Apply to loop. Loop offset shifts the timeline's starting phase.",
		"example": "Movement key 1: Value 3, 0; Duration 2 seconds. Key 2: Value 0, 0; Duration 2 seconds. Repeat On gives a platform that moves three units right, then returns.",
		"tip": "Multi-edit targets the same numbered key on every selected object. Reordering preserves each object's own values. Delay keys have no movement value. Use the native animation inspector for easing/transition controls; playtest to check collisions."},
		{"title": "Groups & saved layouts", "section": "Groups", "picture": "group", "caption": "A named group recalls its selection; it does not merge the objects.",
		"steps": "1. Select objects, enter a Group name and press Create.\n2. Pick a group and use Select to recall its members. Rename changes its label. + Selection/− Selection edit its membership.\n3. Hide group hides it in the editor; Lock group prevents selection/editing. Press again to restore access.\n4. Save groups saves local group data. Saved layouts lets you inspect and remove old saved versions after confirmation.",
		"example": "Select a platform and its spikes, create a group called Bridge, then select that group later to move the whole arrangement together.",
		"tip": "Ungroup removes the group, not its objects. Hiding/locking is editor-only, not a gameplay effect. Groups are matched to the exact saved layout. Capacity: 64 groups per layout, 256 saved layout versions. The open layout cannot be removed; deleting an old record leaves level files untouched."},
		{"title": "Layers & draw order", "section": "Appearance", "picture": "layers", "caption": "A higher custom layer draws in front, even across object types.",
		"steps": "1. Select objects and open Appearance.\n2. Choose Draw layer from −6 to 6 and press its Apply. Higher numbers draw in front; 0 retains native layer ordering.\n3. Use Selection's Back/Backward/Forward/Front to reorder objects within their existing draw bands.\n4. Reset selected visuals removes both custom layers and opacity; Undo can restore them.",
		"example": "A decoration remains behind another object despite pressing Front? Give the decoration layer 1 in Appearance to move it above layer-0 objects.",
		"tip": "Draw layers affect visibility only, not collisions or hazard behavior. Custom layers are saved in level properties but require Goobplayability to render. They do not reorder the game's HUD."},
		{"title": "Opacity", "section": "Appearance", "picture": "opacity", "caption": "100% → 50% → 0% visibility. Collision stays the same.",
		"steps": "1. Select the objects whose appearance you want to change.\n2. Open Appearance and enter Opacity %.\n3. Press the opacity row's Apply. 100 is fully opaque; 0 is invisible.\n4. Set 100 to remove only transparency, or Reset selected visuals to reset opacity and layer together.",
		"example": "Set a background decoration to 35% for a softer look. Keep hazards visible and clear when designing a playable level.",
		"tip": "Making a wall transparent does not let a player pass through it. Mixed means selected objects have different values. Opacity is a Goobplayability visual setting; unmodified clients won't render it."},
		{"title": "Custom laser cycles", "section": "Lasers", "picture": "laser", "caption": "Pause → charge → beam. Charging is part of the active interval.",
		"steps": "1. Select only laser objects, then open Lasers.\n2. Enter Active ticks, Pause ticks and Offset ticks.\n3. Press Apply cycle and start a local playtest to see the real hazard timing.\n4. Reset native cycle removes the custom settings. Undo restores the previous cycle.",
		"example": "Active 90, Pause 30, Offset 0 gives a 120-tick cycle. Give a second laser Offset 60 to shift it by half that cycle. Pause 0 makes a laser always on.",
		"tip": "Ticks are simulation steps, not seconds. Native charge time is retained; active duration is at least charge time + 1 tick. These settings apply only in modded local playtests, not official servers. Changing opacity does not change a laser's danger."},
		{"title": "Level folders & tags", "section": "Library", "picture": "library", "caption": "One local level can belong to a folder and have several tags.",
		"steps": "1. Open Library and Refresh to list local level JSON files.\n2. Click a level. Enter a Folder and comma-separated Tags, then Save labels.\n3. Use the folder dropdown or search names, folders and tags to find levels.\n4. Open selected (or double-click) and confirm. The current level is backed up before another level opens.",
		"example": "Folder: Projects / Racing. Tags: practice, precision. Searching precision will find that level even if its filename doesn't contain that word.",
		"tip": "Folders are virtual labels; files stay in place. This does not organize the online level browser. Clear both fields and Save labels to remove labels. Opening resets undo history; use normal Save to save your level edits. Limits: 16 tags per level and 2000 labeled files."},
		{"title": "Recovery & safe undo", "section": "Recovery", "picture": "recovery", "caption": "A snapshot copies the whole layout; restoring starts fresh undo history.",
		"steps": "1. Use Undo/Redo for recent object edits while the same level is open.\n2. Before a larger experiment, open Recovery and press Save snapshot.\n3. Keep Local autosave enabled for changed-only snapshots every minute. Editor+ also captures before playtest.\n4. Select a snapshot and press Restore selected. Confirm only when you want to replace the current editor layout.",
		"example": "Save a snapshot before rearranging an obstacle section. If several edits go wrong, restore that snapshot instead of undoing each action separately.",
		"tip": "Restoring first backs up the current layout, then resets native undo history. Recovery keeps the latest 20 local snapshots, each up to 2MB. Snapshots are not a substitute for normal Save and do not overwrite original level files."},
		{"title": "Quick reference & limits", "section": "Selection", "picture": "window", "caption": "Corner handle = window size. Scale row = size of text and controls.",
		"steps": "Move window: drag the title. Resize window: drag ↘. Scale interface: − / % / +. Fit: bring it on screen. Reset/Ctrl+0: restore scale, size and position.\n\nUndo: Ctrl+Z. Redo: Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z. Rotate selected shapes: R; Shift+R reverses direction. Numeric fields apply with their Apply button or Enter.",
		"example": "If the menu becomes too large to reach its buttons, leave Editor+ open and press Ctrl+0. If content is cut off in a small window, scroll the middle section or drag ↘ outward.",
		"tip": "Editor+ edits are available while editing, not during playtest. Unsupported selections are rejected rather than partly changed. Keep sub-unit geometry static. Sub-unit geometry/custom visuals need the mod; custom laser timing is local-only. This guide never changes your level."}
	]
